cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use Vacant, or cite the measurements in this repository, please cite it as below."
title: "Vacant: an accountability layer for AI agents"
type: software
version: "0.7.0"
date-released: "2026-09-18"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/cosmopig/Vacant"
url: "https://vacant.cosmopig.com"
authors:
  - name: "cosmopig"
    alias: "cosmopig"
keywords:
  - accountability layer
  - executable acceptance tests
  - AI agent
  - agent harness
  - pre-registered replication
  - signed receipts
  - hash chain
  - Ed25519
  - LLM code generation
  - LiveCodeBench
  - HumanEval+
  - MBPP+
  - McNemar
  - Holm correction
abstract: >-
  Vacant is an accountability layer that sits at the delivery exit of any AI agent -- a receiving
  desk rather than a mandatory wrapper, and not an agent framework: because it inspects only the
  deliverable and not how the agent ran, output from any framework can be fed into it. It runs the
  client's own executable acceptance tests, gates delivery on the result (shipping only what passes and refusing otherwise,
  where a refusal counts as a failure), and signs every attempt into an append-only Ed25519 hash
  chain that anyone holding the public key can re-verify offline, including a multi-party mode in
  which k keys each execute and each sign so that a disagreement names the dissenting key. The
  repository contains the layer itself, the experiment rig, and the pre-registered measurements it
  produced on LLM code generation benchmarks (LiveCodeBench, HumanEval+, MBPP+) with a local 12B
  model: across five same-bank replications and a four-set cross-benchmark run, a revision loop beat
  a single shot in all fifteen replication cells after Holm correction and in every benchmark set
  (pooled p_adj 2.0e-8), while the same loop's advantage over same-budget resampling stayed at
  +0.6 to +5.8 percentage points with the same sign in nine out of nine measurements, none of which
  established it (0 of the 5 same-bank replications passed Holm correction; in the cross-benchmark run
  no per-set uncorrected p reached significance and the pooled Holm-adjusted p was 0.34), so its
  magnitude is reported as unresolved rather than established;
  most of the measured gain is attributable to the executable acceptance gate plus resampling.
  9,841 receipt entries across 67 runs were re-verified entry by entry with zero failures. The
  hidden-test leakage audit reported zero violations on all 67 blocks under its v2 scope, but that
  audit covers only the loop arms: its filter skips any arm outside (HPI, HOC, HMIX), so the
  single-shot and gate arms were never scanned at all, and it additionally skips trivial needles
  (in R460R r1-r3, 57,248 of 95,090 needles were actually checked and 37,842 (39.8%) were skipped).
  A retroactive sweep of the unscanned arms is in progress and its result is not yet in, so no claim
  of hidden-test separation across arms is made here.
  Every delivery-effect figure is stated under the premise that requirements
  can be compiled into executable acceptance tests; where they cannot, the mechanism has no free
  judge. The receipt chain provides integrity, not completeness: a truncated tail is a valid prefix
  and verifies (a truncation/omission attack), so detecting omission requires an exogenous
  commitment such as externally published chain heads. The deliverable of the project is a physical exhibition, not a paper.
